<article class="privacy-policy-vr">
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>

  <p><strong>Effective date:</strong> 06/06/2026</p>
  <p><strong>Application:</strong> VR Physical Rehab</p>
  <p><strong>Controller / Responsible organisation:</strong> Centro Singular de Investigación en Tecnoloxías Intelixentes</p>
  <p><strong>Contact:</strong> <a href="mailto:desarrollovrar@gmail.com">desarrollovrar@gmail.com</a></p>

  <section>
    <h2>1. Purpose of this Privacy Policy</h2>
    <p>This Privacy Policy explains how [VR App Name] collects, uses, stores, and protects personal data when the user interacts with the virtual reality application and its connected server platform.</p>
    <p>The application is designed to support VR-based rehabilitation, monitoring, assessment, research, or training activities. During use, the application communicates with a remote server and may transmit data related to user movement, body position, session execution, app performance, and screen recordings.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Data we collect</h2>
    <p>The application may collect the following categories of data:</p>

    <h3>a. User and session information</h3>
    <p>We may collect identifiers required to associate the VR session with the correct user, patient, participant, therapist, clinical session, routine, or exercise plan.</p>
    <p>This may include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>User or participant ID.</li>
      <li>Session ID.</li>
      <li>Routine or exercise ID.</li>
      <li>Device ID.</li>
      <li>Date and time of the session.</li>
      <li>Session status, such as started, paused, resumed, stopped, finished, or rebooted.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>b. Body position and movement data</h3>
    <p>The application may collect movement and positional data generated during the VR session.</p>
    <p>This may include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Head position and rotation.</li>
      <li>Hand position and rotation.</li>
      <li>Controller or hand-tracking data.</li>
      <li>Movement trajectories.</li>
      <li>Exercise execution data.</li>
      <li>Detected posture or movement patterns.</li>
      <li>Success, failure, missed movement, timing, score, or performance outcome.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This data is used to evaluate exercise execution, monitor rehabilitation activity, generate reports, and improve the functionality of the system.</p>

    <h3>c. App performance and technical data</h3>
    <p>The application may collect technical information necessary to maintain, debug, and improve the system.</p>
    <p>This may include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Device type and device identifier.</li>
      <li>Application version.</li>
      <li>Operating system or platform version.</li>
      <li>Server connection status.</li>
      <li>Network or communication logs.</li>
      <li>Error logs.</li>
      <li>Frame rate, latency, crash reports, or app performance metrics.</li>
      <li>MQTT or server command status logs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>d. Screen recordings or VR session recordings</h3>
    <p>The application may record or transmit the VR screen, user viewpoint, or session video for monitoring, clinical review, technical support, validation, training, or research purposes.</p>
    <p>Screen recordings may contain:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The user’s VR point of view.</li>
      <li>Exercise execution.</li>
      <li>Interface elements.</li>
      <li>Session status.</li>
      <li>Potentially identifiable information shown inside the application.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Screen recording will only be used for the purposes described in this policy and, where required, with explicit consent.</p>

    <h3>e. Health-related or rehabilitation data</h3>
    <p>Where the application is used in a medical, rehabilitation, clinical, or research context, the data collected may reveal information about the user’s physical condition, rehabilitation progress, motor performance, limitations, or treatment plan.</p>
    <p>This type of information may be considered health-related personal data and may require additional legal safeguards.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. How we collect data</h2>
    <p>Data may be collected directly through the VR headset, controllers, hand-tracking systems, movement sensors, in-app interactions, server communications, and session recordings.</p>
    <p>The application may communicate with the server using secure API requests and real-time messaging protocols. This communication may include downloading assigned sessions, receiving commands from the server, and uploading exercise results or technical logs.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Why we process the data</h2>
    <p>We process data for the following purposes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>To run the VR rehabilitation or training session.</li>
      <li>To assign exercises and routines to the correct user.</li>
      <li>To measure exercise execution and movement performance.</li>
      <li>To allow clinicians, therapists, researchers, or authorised staff to review session results.</li>
      <li>To generate reports, statistics, graphs, and progress indicators.</li>
      <li>To detect errors, crashes, connection problems, or performance issues.</li>
      <li>To improve the usability, safety, and reliability of the application.</li>
      <li>To support research, validation, or clinical evaluation, where applicable.</li>
      <li>To maintain security and prevent unauthorised access.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We do not use the data for advertising or unrelated commercial profiling.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. Legal basis for processing</h2>
    <p>Depending on the context in which the application is used, the legal basis for processing may include one or more of the following:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The user’s explicit consent.</li>
      <li>The provision of healthcare, rehabilitation, or related services.</li>
      <li>Scientific research purposes, subject to appropriate safeguards.</li>
      <li>Compliance with legal or institutional obligations.</li>
      <li>Legitimate interest in maintaining the security, reliability, and performance of the system, where this does not override the rights and freedoms of the user.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where the data includes health-related data, biometric data, or other sensitive data, it will be processed only where a valid legal basis and an applicable special-category condition are present.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. Who can access the data</h2>
    <p>Access to the data is limited to authorised persons who need it for the purposes described in this policy.</p>
    <p>This may include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Healthcare professionals.</li>
      <li>Therapists or rehabilitation staff.</li>
      <li>Researchers involved in the project.</li>
      <li>Technical administrators.</li>
      <li>System maintainers.</li>
      <li>Data protection or compliance personnel, where required.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Access is controlled using authentication, authorisation, and role-based permissions where applicable.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>7. Data sharing</h2>
    <p>We do not sell personal data.</p>
    <p>Data may be shared only when necessary with:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The institution or organisation responsible for the rehabilitation, research, or training activity.</li>
      <li>Technical service providers that host, maintain, or support the server infrastructure.</li>
      <li>Research partners, only where permitted and subject to appropriate safeguards.</li>
      <li>Public authorities, regulators, or ethics committees, where legally required.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where possible, research or reporting data will be anonymised or pseudonymised before being used for analysis.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>8. International data transfers</h2>
    <p>Data is stored and processed in [Country / Region].</p>
    <p>If data is transferred outside the European Economic Area or another applicable jurisdiction, appropriate safeguards will be applied, such as adequacy decisions, standard contractual clauses, or other legally recognised mechanisms.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>9. Data retention</h2>
    <p>Personal data will be kept only for as long as necessary for the purposes described in this policy. GDPR guidance states that personal data should be stored for the shortest time possible, taking into account the purpose of processing and legal retention obligations.</p>
    <p>Retention periods may depend on:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The duration of the rehabilitation programme.</li>
      <li>Clinical, research, or institutional requirements.</li>
      <li>Legal or ethical obligations.</li>
      <li>Technical maintenance or security needs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Suggested retention periods:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Session and exercise data: [X months/years].</li>
      <li>Technical logs: [X days/months].</li>
      <li>Screen recordings: [X days/months].</li>
      <li>Research datasets: [X years], preferably anonymised or pseudonymised.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After the retention period, data will be deleted, anonymised, or securely archived according to applicable rules.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>10. Security measures</h2>
    <p>We use appropriate technical and organisational measures to protect personal data.</p>
    <p>These may include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Encrypted communication between the VR app and the server.</li>
      <li>Authentication tokens or secure device authorisation.</li>
      <li>Access control for authorised users.</li>
      <li>Server-side logging and monitoring.</li>
      <li>Secure storage of credentials.</li>
      <li>Pseudonymisation where possible.</li>
      <li>Regular review of access permissions.</li>
      <li>Protection against unauthorised access, loss, misuse, or alteration.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>No system can guarantee absolute security, but we take reasonable measures to reduce risk.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>11. User rights</h2>
    <p>Depending on applicable law, users may have the right to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Access their personal data.</li>
      <li>Request correction of inaccurate data.</li>
      <li>Request deletion of data.</li>
      <li>Restrict or object to processing.</li>
      <li>Withdraw consent, where processing is based on consent.</li>
      <li>Request data portability, where applicable.</li>
      <li>Lodge a complaint with a data protection authority.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To exercise these rights, contact: [Contact email].</p>
    <p>Withdrawal of consent will not affect processing that occurred before consent was withdrawn.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>12. Screen recording notice</h2>
    <p>The application may record the VR screen or user point of view during a session.</p>
    <p>Screen recording is used for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Clinical or therapist review.</li>
      <li>Technical debugging.</li>
      <li>Research validation.</li>
      <li>Quality control.</li>
      <li>Training or documentation, where authorised.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Screen recordings may contain sensitive information. Access to recordings is restricted. Recordings will not be shared publicly or used for unrelated purposes without additional authorisation or consent.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>13. Children or vulnerable users</h2>
    <p>If the application is used by minors, patients with cognitive impairment, or other vulnerable users, consent or authorisation may be required from a parent, legal guardian, authorised representative, ethics committee, or healthcare institution.</p>
    <p>Additional safeguards may be applied to protect these users.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>14. Automated analysis</h2>
    <p>The application may automatically process movement and performance data to classify exercise outcomes, detect patterns, calculate scores, or generate rehabilitation indicators.</p>
    <p>These analyses are intended to support human review and should not be treated as a standalone medical diagnosis unless expressly validated and authorised for that purpose.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>15. Changes to this Privacy Policy</h2>
    <p>We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. Any relevant changes will be made available through the application, website, institution, or project documentation.</p>
  </section>

